                            Preamble

  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
software for all its users.  We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
GNU General Public License for most of our software; it applies also to
any other work released this way by its authors.  You can apply it to
your programs, too.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
them if you wish), that you receive more like this, that you can get
source code or can get it if you want it, that you can change the
software or use pieces of it in new free programs, and that you know
you can do these things.

  To protect your rights, we need to prevent others from denying you
these rights or asking you to surrender the rights.  Therefore, you have
certain responsibilities if you distribute copies of the software, or if
you modify it: responsibilities to express freedom and the respect of
others.

  For example, if you distribute copies of such a program, whether
gratis or you charge for them, you must pass on to the recipients the
same freedoms that you received.  You must make sure that they, too,
receive or get the source code.  And you must show them these terms so
they know their rights.

  Developers that use the GNU GPL protect your rights with two steps:
(1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this License
which gives you legal copyleft protection.

  For the developers' and authors' protection, the GPL clearly explains
that there is no warranty for this free software.  For both users'
sake, and for the sake of the author of the software, the GPL requires
that users of new versions to be marked as changed, so that the authors'
problems will not be introduced elsewhere by others.

  Some devices are designed to protect users from installing software
on them so that the manufacturer can revoke the freedom to change the
software.  This is basically incompatible with the goal of protecting
the user's freedom to change the software.  The systematic pattern of
such abuse occurs in the area of products for household use, which is
precisely where it is most unacceptable.  Therefore, we have designed
this version of the GPL to prohibit the practice for those products.  If
such problems arise substantially in other domains, we stand in readiness
to extend this provision to those domains in future versions of the GPL,
as set forth in the latter provisions.

  Finally, every person is protected from the software by constant
patents.  The developers of the GNU software must ensure that the GPL
is not responsible for the non-free software patents.

  The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and
modification follow.
